# The nightly loop's schedule.  COPY THIS, EDIT IT, INSTALL IT.
#
#   crontab -l > crontab.before                      # back up first
#   crontab tools/pengyos-nightly/crontab.example    # installs THIS file wholesale
#   diff crontab.before <(crontab -l)                # verify the WHOLE file
#
# ⚠ `crontab <file>` REPLACES your entire crontab. If you already have jobs, merge
#   this file into `crontab -l` output and install the merged result. Verify a
#   crontab edit by diffing the whole file — never by grepping for the lines you
#   care about. On 2026-10-17 a REPLACE silently dropped two nightly lines, both
#   loops stopped for a day, and the staleness alarm fired ~15 h late naming the
#   wrong cause. See ../docs/operations.md §9.
#
# Replace <repo>, <build-root>, <state-dir> and <site-dir> with your paths.
# Set act_time in <state-dir>/nightly.toml to match the act line below.

# ─── environment the tooling expects ──────────────────────────────────────────
HOME=/home/YOU
PATH=/home/YOU/.local/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
PENGYOS_REPO=<repo>
PENGYOS_BUILD_ROOT=<build-root>
PENGYOS_SITE=<site-dir>

# ─── PLAN: observe, triage, report, write plan.json ────────────────────────────
# Read-only against the tree, so it is safe to run while you are working.
0 19 * * * cd <repo>/tools/pengyos-nightly && /usr/bin/env python3 -m pengyos_nightly --repo <repo> --build-root <build-root> --state-dir <state-dir> --site <site-dir> run >> $HOME/logs/cron/pengyos-nightly.log 2>&1

# ─── ACT: apply the night's batch into the TESTING channel ─────────────────────
# PUT THIS IN A WINDOW WHERE NOTHING ELSE TOUCHES THE CHECKOUT.
#
# The act lane's first guard is clean_tree: it refuses to run on a checkout with
# uncommitted changes, because a refusal once destroyed an agent job's uncommitted
# work. So the act lane is scheduled against your own working hours — schedule it
# at 21:00, be working at 21:00, and the guard refuses and the night is lost.
#
# Pick a quiet window and check three things:
#   - no agent job, build, or interactive session touches <repo> at that hour
#   - the machine does not suspend at that hour (systemctl is-suspend-target)
#   - the plan is hours older, on the next calendar day. That is fine:
#     release_cap counts releases published *today*, and `act` re-plans when
#     decisions.json is newer than plan.json, so a wider gap gives approvals
#     MORE time to land, not less.
0 2 * * * cd <repo>/tools/pengyos-nightly && /usr/bin/env python3 -m pengyos_nightly --repo <repo> --build-root <build-root> --state-dir <state-dir> --site <site-dir> act --apply >> $HOME/logs/cron/pengyos-nightly-act.log 2>&1

# ─── the probe: silence is a design feature, so probe it ───────────────────────
# "Nothing new" and "nothing allowed" are HEALTHY and silent, and indistinguishable
# from a loop that is broken, removed from the crontab, or on a machine that is off.
# status exits 1 when the loop is actually stuck, so a watchdog can use it.
30 * * * * cd <repo>/tools/pengyos-nightly && /usr/bin/env python3 -m pengyos_nightly --repo <repo> --build-root <build-root> --state-dir <state-dir> --site <site-dir> status >> $HOME/logs/cron/pengyos-nightly-status.log 2>&1

# ─── assert the crontab's CONTENT, not just the log's age ──────────────────────
# A cadence check that ages a log goes red only after the cadence window, and cannot
# say "the entry was removed" — a removed entry has no log to age. This fires within
# an hour of an edit that drops the nightly lines.
15 * * * * crontab -l | grep -c 'pengyos_nightly .* run' | grep -qx 1 && crontab -l | grep -c 'pengyos_nightly .* act --apply' | grep -qx 1 || echo "CRON LEDGER: a pengyos-nightly entry is MISSING from the crontab" >> $HOME/logs/cron/cron-ledger.log
